From 79d28c5b0a144b1f9b01f14dc980c03fa7bb0443 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: akapug <akapug@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 18:09:31 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 13/16] sycl: IQ4_XS reorder layout, read by the XMX mat-mul at
 every column count

IQ4_XS had no reorder layout, so the XMX mat-mul read its 136-byte blocks as they are and
each lane's block straddled cache lines. Add one: [qs 128][d, scales_h, scales_l] regions over
all blocks, which leaves the codes line-aligned. Only the XMX mat-mul reads it, so a weight
moves to it only where that path will serve it (a large 2-D weight whose rows come in groups of
16, on a device with XMX, not a split buffer), and it is then served on XMX from one column.
A reordered IQ4_XS that reaches the dp4a dispatch stops on an assertion; prefill dequantizes
the new layout. IQ4_XS gate/up pairs keep the fused dp4a kernel, which is still faster at
one column than two XMX launches.

The layout is taken only from 32M values. XMX is as fast or faster at one column above that,
and Qwen3-4B's 25M-value weights, although faster alone too, decoded 0.8% slower at one stream
with them on XMX.

Arc Pro B70, test-backend-ops perf, IQ4_XS, dp4a -> XMX, us at 1 / 2 / 4 / 8 columns:
  5120x17408  145 / 160 / 185 / 259 -> 127 / 128 / 129 / 131
  17408x5120  137 / 149 / 177 / 245 -> 137 / 137 / 139 / 141
Qwen3-4B UD-Q4_K_XL, llama-batched-bench decode: 1 stream unchanged, 4 streams +3%.
test-backend-ops MUL_MAT, MUL_MAT_VEC_FUSION and reorder-then-batch pass against the CPU;
adds IQ4_XS cases above the size threshold, as a mat-mul at 1, 4 and 8 tokens and as a
prefill after a reorder.
